## Plan

### 1. Scrub `runInit` of the brain.aide scaffold step and brain-shaped option

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/folder-structure`

- [ ] 1a. Delete the `options: { brainPath?: string } = {}` parameter from `runInit`'s signature in `src/cli/init/index.ts`. The new shape is `runInit(cwd: string, write: (line: string) => void = ...): Promise<number>`. Update the JSDoc above the function to remove every reference to `brainPath`, `<BRAIN_PATH>`, and the brain.aide seed-write step.
- [ ] 1b. Delete the entire brain.aide scaffold block: `brainAidePath`, the `let brainAideExists` flag, the `try { await access ... } catch {}` block, and the entire `brainAideResult` `InstallResult` IIFE-with-mkdir-writeFile that produces the `[created]` or `[exists]` brain.aide line. The scaffold step is gone; nothing inside `runInit` ever touches `.aide/config/`.
- [ ] 1c. In the `results: InstallResult[] = [...]` initialization, drop the `brainAideResult` element. The new initial value is `[mcpResult]` — the MCP entry result is the first artifact in the per-file log; planned-step results follow.
- [ ] 1d. Replace the call site `const mcpRaw = await writeMcpEntry(cwd, options.brainPath)` with `const mcpRaw = await writeMcpEntry(cwd)`. The helper's signature shrinks to one positional argument (Step 2 covers the helper itself). Keep the `mcpResult: InstallResult = { ...mcpRaw, displayPath: ".mcp.json" }` mapping unchanged.
- [ ] 1e. Remove now-unused imports from `src/cli/init/index.ts`. The `import obsidianBrainAideTemplate ...` line goes. From `node:fs/promises` keep nothing brain-related — verify whether `writeFile`, `access`, `mkdir` are still referenced elsewhere in the file (they are not — the scaffold block was their only consumer); delete those named imports. The `dirname` import from `node:path` similarly becomes orphaned — delete it. Keep `join` (still used for `versionsHostPath` and the planning helpers' path computations).

### 2. Strip brain logic out of `writeMcpEntry`

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow`

- [ ] 2a. In `src/cli/init/writeMcpEntry/index.ts`, change the function signature to `writeMcpEntry(projectRoot: string): Promise<WriteMcpEntryResult>`. The second `brainPath` parameter is removed end-to-end; no successor parameter replaces it.
- [ ] 2b. Delete the entire brain-derivation section: the `brainAidePath = join(...)` line, the `try { brainAideContent = await readFile ... } catch (err) { ... }` block, the call to `parseBrainAideFromString`, the throw on non-`ok` parse, the construction of `brainEntry: McpServerEntry` via `interpolateArgs`, the assignment `entries.brain = brainEntry`, and the assignment `entries.obsidian = "delete"`. The `entries` map is now exactly one literal: `const entries: Record<string, McpServerEntry | "delete"> = { aide: mcpEntry() }`.
- [ ] 2c. Delete the now-unused imports: `readFile` from `node:fs/promises`, `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`, `parseBrainAideFromString` and `interpolateArgs` from `@/service/parseBrainAide`. Keep `join` only if still needed for the preserved-count read; check after the edit and remove if not. Keep `mcpEntry` from `wireMcp`. Keep `sharedWriteMcpEntry` from `@/cli/shared/writeMcpEntry`. Keep `McpServerEntry` from `@/types` if it's still used in the type annotation; remove if not.
- [ ] 2d. Update the message-composition logic at the bottom of the function. Today it composes `"aide and brain MCP server entries"` and adds a "removed legacy obsidian key" suffix. With only the `aide` entry in play:
    - On the `result.unchanged` branch return `{ status: "exists", message: "aide MCP server entry already configured" }`.
    - On the write branch, `written` will be `["aide"]` (single-entry singular form: `"aide MCP server entry"`). `deleted` will be empty (no `obsidian: "delete"` is sent). Preserve the existing "merged with N existing server(s)" suffix logic — that detects non-managed pre-existing servers from the file on disk after the write — and update the `managedKeys` set to `new Set(["aide", "aidemd-mcp"])` (drop `"brain"` and `"obsidian"` since the cold-start CLI no longer manages those keys). Final message shape: `"aide MCP server entry"` or `"aide MCP server entry (merged with 2 existing servers)"`.
- [ ] 2e. Rewrite the JSDoc on the default export. The old JSDoc describes a 3-step pipeline (mcp entry, brain derivation, delegate). The new JSDoc must say:
    - The helper writes ONLY the `aide` key into `.mcp.json` via the shared additive-merge writer.
    - It never reads `.aide/config/brain.aide`, never derives a brain entry, never writes a `brain` key, never deletes a legacy `obsidian` key. Brain MCP wiring is owned exclusively by `cli/sync` invoked from `/aide:brain config` after the integration's prose has scaffolded `brain.aide`.
    - Throws on malformed `.mcp.json` (the only abort trigger for the cold-start CLI).
    - Idempotent: re-running on a project where the `aide` entry is already present returns `{ status: "exists", ... }` with no write.

### 3. Rewrite `deferredCategories` and the per-file log message contract

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `coding-playbook/architecture/data-flow`

- [ ] 3a. Replace the body of `deferredCategories(brainPath)` in `src/cli/init/index.ts` with a parameter-less helper. Remove the `brainPath` parameter entirely. The helper returns the same array on every call, in the order the spec's good example shows:

    1. `"Brain wiring — open Claude Code and run /aide; on the first run, /aide:brain config will scaffold .aide/config/brain.aide, ask for whatever your chosen backend needs, derive the brain MCP entry, and emit a restart message."`
    2. `"IDE configuration — re-run: npx aidemd-mcp init --ide <choice>"`

    The brain entry is now the always-present-on-cold-install kind, not the placeholder kind, because the cold-start CLI never writes any brain artifact. The IDE entry is preserved verbatim from the current implementation. (The exact strings are load-bearing for tests in Steps 6 and 8.)
- [ ] 3b. Update the JSDoc above `deferredCategories` to describe the new contract:
    - Brain wiring is permanently routed to `/aide:brain config` on the first `/aide` run regardless of CLI flags or disk state — the cold-start CLI does not write any brain artifact.
    - Naming `/aide` *itself* as the brain follow-up is forbidden — the orchestrator forwards brain wiring to `/aide:brain config` and does not own inline recovery. The good-example string above does point at `/aide` as the entry point the user types in Claude Code, then names `/aide:brain config` as the surface that does the wiring; this is consistent with the spec's example output and with `/aide`'s role as the single Claude-Code entry the user invokes. The forbidden shape is naming `/aide` as the *wiring* surface (e.g., "the orchestrator will detect that your brain is not wired and walk you through configuration"). The shipped string names `/aide:brain config` as the wiring surface — that is the regression guard.
    - Cite the spec's `outcomes.undesired` "directs the user to a removed slash command" entry as the rule this guards against.
- [ ] 3c. Remove the `deferredCategories(brainPath)` call site arguments at the warning-construction site (`renderWarning(...)` near the bottom of `runInit`). Replace the existing `deferredCategories: deferredCategories(options.brainPath)` field with `deferredCategories: deferredCategories()`.

### 4. Rewrite the IIFE flag-parsing surface and `--help` text

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

- [ ] 4a. In `src/cli/init/index.ts`'s top-level IIFE:
    - Delete the `parseBrainPath` function entirely.
    - Delete the line `const brainPath = parseBrainPath(process.argv.slice(2))` and the `{ brainPath }` argument passed into `runInit`. The new call site is `await runInit(process.cwd())`. The `--help` early-exit branch stays; the `try { ... } catch { ... }` still wraps `runInit` so malformed `.mcp.json` still maps to `process.stderr.write(...)` + `process.exit(1)`.
- [ ] 4b. Rewrite the `--help` block. The new text must:
    - State the usage shape: `npx aidemd-mcp init` (no flags listed beyond `--help` in this plan's shipped surface).
    - Describe what the CLI installs: methodology pointer stub, methodology doc set under `.aide/docs/`, every pipeline slash command, every pipeline agent, every skill template, the `aide` MCP server entry merged additively into `.mcp.json`, the `aide-tree` launcher, and the README badge.
    - Explicitly state what the CLI does NOT do: never writes `.aide/config/brain.aide`, never writes a `.mcp.json` `brain` entry, never asks for a brain path, never invokes `provisionBrain`, never creates the brain root directory, never accepts any brain-shaped flag.
    - State the post-install follow-up surfaces: brain wiring → run `/aide:brain config` on the first `/aide` run inside Claude Code; deferred IDE → re-run with `--ide <choice>`; skipped shipped artifacts → run `/aide:upgrade`.
    - Drop every reference to `--brain-path`, `--vault-path`, `<BRAIN_PATH>`, `npx aidemd-mcp sync` for brain wiring (sync is still part of the `/aide:brain config` flow but the CLI's `--help` does not need to advertise that — it is owned by the wiring prose).
- [ ] 4c. Verify with Grep that no other module under `src/cli/init/` references `parseBrainPath`, `parseVaultPath`, `--brain-path`, `--vault-path`, `brainPath`, or `vaultPath` after Step 1–4 land. Any straggler references in tests are addressed in Steps 5–8.

### 5. Rewrite `writeMcpEntry/index.test.ts`

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`

- [ ] 5a. Delete every test that exercises brain-derivation or brain-entry-write semantics. Concretely, remove the test blocks labelled `2a`, `2b`, `2c`, `2e`, the `8e` deprecated-field-rejection block, the "brain entry args contain the real brain root path" assertion, and any test whose body sets up `.aide/config/brain.aide` content via `writeFile`+`mkdir` or whose assertions inspect `servers.brain` / `servers.obsidian`.
- [ ] 5b. Add the new test set covering the methodology-only contract:
    - `writeMcpEntry(tempDir)` on a directory with no `.mcp.json` writes a file whose `mcpServers` map has exactly one key, `aide`, with the bytes returned by `mcpEntry()`. The result is `{ status: "created", message: "aide MCP server entry" }`.
    - `writeMcpEntry(tempDir)` on a directory that already has `mcpServers.aide` returns `{ status: "exists", message: "aide MCP server entry already configured" }` and does not modify the file (snapshot bytes before and after the call).
    - `writeMcpEntry(tempDir)` on a directory that has `mcpServers.github` and a custom `mcpServers.local-llm` entry merges additively: post-call the file has `aide`, `github`, `local-llm` — three keys, `github` and `local-llm` byte-identical to their pre-call shape. Result message contains the `(merged with 2 existing servers)` suffix.
    - `writeMcpEntry(tempDir)` on a directory that already has a legacy `mcpServers.obsidian` entry (from a pre-rework install) leaves the legacy `obsidian` key UNTOUCHED. The cold-start CLI no longer migrates legacy keys; that responsibility belongs to `/aide:brain config` (which can decide what to do with the legacy key in the user's session). Assert `servers.obsidian` is still present after the call. Assert `servers.brain` does NOT exist post-call. (See Decisions for why this changed.)
    - `writeMcpEntry(tempDir)` on a directory that already has `mcpServers.brain` (e.g., from a prior `/aide:brain config` run that ran sync) leaves `mcpServers.brain` UNTOUCHED. The cold-start CLI does not write, modify, or delete the `brain` key under any circumstance.
    - `writeMcpEntry(tempDir)` against a `.mcp.json` containing malformed JSON throws an error whose message contains `".mcp.json"` and `"invalid JSON"`. The test mirrors the existing `throws on malformed JSON` test — keep that block, just remove its dependence on brain logic if any.
    - `writeMcpEntry(tempDir)` writes JSON with two-space indent and a trailing newline. Preserve the existing format-fidelity test verbatim — it does not depend on brain logic.
    - `writeMcpEntry(tempDir)` preserves top-level keys outside `mcpServers` (preserve the existing `someOtherKey` test verbatim).
- [ ] 5c. Delete the `expectedBrainEntry` helper function and any imports of `obsidianBrainAideTemplate`, `parseBrainAideFromString`, `interpolateArgs`. Keep imports for `mcpEntry`, the file-system helpers (`mkdtemp`, `readFile`, `writeFile`, `mkdir`, `rm`), `path/os/tmpdir` setup, and the helper `readMcpJson`. Result: the test file imports zero brain-related symbols.
- [ ] 5d. Verify the test file's `describe("writeMcpEntry (init wrapper)", ...)` describe label still applies — the helper is still the init wrapper, only its responsibility shrank. Keep the label.

### 6. Rewrite `index.test.ts` (unit tests with mocks)

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`

- [ ] 6a. Delete every `vi.mock` declaration related to brain scaffolding. Specifically remove the `vi.mock("@/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.js", ...)` line and the `vi.mock("node:fs/promises", ...)` block (the orchestrator no longer calls `writeFile`, `access`, or `mkdir` directly — those calls were brain-only). Remove the `mockObsidianBrainAideTemplate`, `mockWriteFile`, `mockAccess` typed handles. The other `vi.mock` calls (`writeMcpEntry`, `renderWarning`, `detectFramework`, every install helper, `compareBytes`, `readVersionsManifest`, `applySteps`) stay — they cover the methodology-layer pipeline.
- [ ] 6b. Update the canonical fixture at the top of the file:
    - Delete `DEFERRED_CATEGORIES_WITH_BRAIN_PATH` entirely — it has no callers anymore.
    - Replace `DEFERRED_CATEGORIES` with the single new array containing the two entries from Step 3a (brain wiring routes to `/aide:brain config` on first `/aide` run; IDE re-run). The fixture is the single source of truth for the expected `renderWarning` input across every test in this file.
    - Update `mockWriteMcpEntry.mockResolvedValue(...)` to match the new result shape: `{ status: "created", message: "aide MCP server entry" }` on the happy path; tests that need the idempotent path override per-test.
- [ ] 6c. Delete every test block that covers a retired contract. Concretely: every `5b-i`, `5b-ii`, `5c`, `3c`, `3d`, `5e`, `5e-new`, `5f` block — they all test the `<BRAIN_PATH>` placeholder, the always-scaffold contract, and the `brainPath` flag forms, all of which are gone. Delete the `cold-start happy path`'s "Brain.aide scaffold is first" assertion line; the per-file log no longer carries a brain.aide line at all.
- [ ] 6d. Add the new test blocks proving the no-brain-surfaces contract. The `runInit` signature is now `(cwd, write?)` — every test calls it with two arguments at most. New blocks:
    - **6d-i. Cold-start happy path**: every planning helper returns `would-create` steps, `applySteps` flips them to `created`. Assert:
        - The per-file log's first line is `[created] .mcp.json — aide MCP server entry` (the brain.aide line is gone). Subsequent lines log every planned step.
        - No log line ever mentions `brain.aide`, `<BRAIN_PATH>`, or `.aide/config/`.
        - `mockObsidianBrainAideTemplate` is uninvoked (it is no longer imported by the orchestrator; verify by Grep that the test does not import it either — see 6a).
        - `mockWriteMcpEntry` was called with exactly one positional argument: `cwd`. No `brainPath` value is passed.
        - `renderWarning` receives `{ skipped: [], failed: [], deferredCategories: <the two-item array fixture> }`.
        - `runInit` returns `0`.
    - **6d-ii. Idempotent re-run**: every helper returns `exists`. Assert:
        - Every log line begins with `[exists]`.
        - No log line mentions `brain.aide`.
        - `renderWarning` still receives the two-item deferredCategories array (the brain wiring follow-up does not depend on disk state).
        - `runInit` returns `0`.
    - **6d-iii. Mixed run (some `would-create`, some `exists`, some `would-overwrite`)**: existing patterns already cover the partition; preserve them but update the expected log lines to drop the brain.aide line.
    - **6d-iv. Abort path**: when `writeMcpEntry` rejects (malformed `.mcp.json`), `runInit` rejects with the same error, no other helper runs after the throw. Preserve the existing assertions but verify that `mockObsidianBrainAideTemplate` is not in the imports/assertions list anymore.
    - **6d-v. Invocation ordering**: `writeMcpEntry` runs before every planner. The brain.aide ordering assertion is dropped — there is no brain.aide step.
    - **6d-vi (new — regression guard)**: `runInit` is called with two arguments: `cwd` and a `write` callback. Calling it with a third positional argument should be a TypeScript-time error; assert at runtime that `runInit.length === 2`. (`Function.length` returns the number of declared parameters minus the count with default values; with `cwd` (required) and `write` (defaulted), `length === 1`. Adjust the assertion to whichever value the new signature produces — the point is that no third parameter exists.)
    - **6d-vii (new — regression guard)**: across every non-abort test, assert `mockWriteMcpEntry` is invoked with exactly one argument (length-1 call). The new signature `writeMcpEntry(cwd)` no longer accepts a second positional. Use `expect(mockWriteMcpEntry).toHaveBeenCalledWith(CWD)` (single-arg matcher) or `mockWriteMcpEntry.mock.calls[0]` length assertion.
    - **6d-viii (new — regression guard)**: across every non-abort test, assert no log line and no `renderWarning` deferred string contains the substring `<BRAIN_PATH>`. The placeholder framing is gone; this guards against regressions that re-introduce it.
    - **6d-ix (new — regression guard)**: across every non-abort test, assert no `renderWarning` deferred string starts with `Brain wiring — .aide/config/brain.aide was scaffolded with a <BRAIN_PATH> placeholder` (the retired Stage-1-and-earlier prose). The new brain item starts with `"Brain wiring — open Claude Code and run /aide; on the first run, /aide:brain config will"`.
- [ ] 6e. Update every renamed/dropped fixture name in surviving tests. Tests previously named `3c`, `3d`, `5e`, etc. should be renumbered to fit the new structure (`6d-i` through `6d-ix`); naming is the architect's preference but every test must still trace to a behavior the new spec or this plan asserts.
- [ ] 6f. Verify `vi.clearAllMocks()` in `beforeEach` still applies; it does — no change.

### 7. Rewrite `index.integration.test.ts` (real-FS end-to-end tests)

Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`

- [ ] 7a. Delete every test block that creates or asserts on `.aide/config/brain.aide` content. Concretely: delete the `6a`, `6b`, `6c`, `6d`, `6e`, `6f` blocks in their entirety. They all hinge on the always-scaffold contract.
- [ ] 7b. Delete the `parseBrainAide` import and the `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` import — both become unused.
- [ ] 7c. Add the new test blocks asserting the no-brain-surfaces invariant on real disk:
    - **7c-i. Cold install happy path on a fresh `mkdtemp` directory.**
        - Run `runInit(tempDir, write)` (two args, no options). Assert exit `0`.
        - Assert `.aide/config/brain.aide` does NOT exist on disk after the run (use `fs.access` and expect ENOENT).
        - Assert `.aide/config/` does NOT exist as a directory after the run (use `fs.access` on the directory path and expect ENOENT). The CLI must not even create the parent directory for a brain config it never wrote.
        - Assert `.mcp.json` exists; parse it; assert `mcpServers` has exactly the `aide` key and no `brain`, no `obsidian`, no other keys (assuming `mcpServers` was empty before the run).
        - Assert the captured stdout warning contains `"Brain wiring — open Claude Code and run /aide;"` and `"/aide:brain config"`. Assert the warning does NOT contain `<BRAIN_PATH>`, does NOT contain `.aide/config/brain.aide was scaffolded`, does NOT mention `obsidian` or any backend-specific value.
    - **7c-ii. Cold install on a directory with pre-existing `.mcp.json` containing `github` and a legacy `obsidian` entry.**
        - Pre-write `.mcp.json` with `mcpServers: { github: {...}, obsidian: {...} }` and any other top-level keys.
        - Run `runInit(tempDir, write)`.
        - Parse the post-run `.mcp.json`. Assert `mcpServers.aide` exists. Assert `mcpServers.github` is byte-identical to its pre-run shape. Assert `mcpServers.obsidian` is STILL PRESENT and byte-identical (cold-start CLI does not migrate legacy keys; that responsibility moved to `/aide:brain config`). Assert `mcpServers.brain` does NOT exist.
        - Assert top-level keys outside `mcpServers` survive byte-for-byte.
    - **7c-iii. Idempotent re-run.**
        - Run `runInit(tempDir, write)` twice in succession.
        - Snapshot `.mcp.json` bytes between calls; assert byte-equal across the two runs.
        - Assert the second run's stdout has no `[created]` lines; every artifact reports `[exists]`.
        - Assert the warning still contains the brain-wiring deferred entry on the second run (the entry is permanent, not flag-driven).
    - **7c-iv. Pre-existing `.aide/config/brain.aide` on disk.**
        - Pre-write `.aide/config/brain.aide` with arbitrary user content (e.g., the literal string `"# user content — do not touch\n"`).
        - Run `runInit(tempDir, write)`.
        - Read `.aide/config/brain.aide` bytes after the run; assert byte-identical to the pre-run content. The CLI must not touch any file under `.aide/config/`, even if the file is malformed, drifted, or otherwise inconsistent with the canonical Obsidian template.
        - Assert no log line mentions `brain.aide`.
- [ ] 7d. Preserve the `vi.hoisted` `process.exit` interception block at the top of the file — it is still required because the IIFE fires at import time.
- [ ] 7e. Preserve the `mkdtemp` / `rm` lifecycle (`beforeEach` / `afterEach`).

