## Decisions

- **Cold-start CLI is brain-blind, not brain-aware.** The simplest expression of the rewritten spec is "the CLI does not know what a brain is." That means: no helper named after a brain artifact ever runs, no flag carries a brain-shaped value, no log line mentions `.aide/config/`, no `--help` line teaches brain backends, and no test fixture mocks a brain-related dependency. Every failure mode the rewritten spec's `outcomes.undesired` enumerates traces back to the CLI knowing too much about brains; making it know nothing is the structural fix. This decision is the architect-level rule that drives every step in the plan.

- **Drop `brainPath` end-to-end without a back-compat alias.** The retired `--brain-path` flag has no migration story — the value it accepted (an absolute filesystem path inlined into `mcpServerConfig.args`) is now a per-host concern owned by `/aide:brain config`. A back-compat alias would force the CLI to accept the value, then ignore it, then warn — three behaviors with three competing user mental models. The cleaner path is hard-fail at the argv layer: anyone passing `--brain-path` after this lands gets the standard "unknown option" path the IIFE already produces (no flag is recognized except `--help`, so any other arg is ignored — actually a worse failure shape than a hard error, but stays consistent with the existing CLI's lenient flag posture). The migration cost is acceptable because the package is pre-1.0 and the rewritten spec explicitly notes the brain-shaped flag class is forbidden by construction.

- **Cold-start CLI no longer migrates the legacy `obsidian` key.** The current `writeMcpEntry` wraps `entries.obsidian = "delete"` to remove a legacy `obsidian` MCP entry from pre-rework installs. After this plan, the CLI does not touch the `obsidian` key — it survives. Two reasons: (1) the rewritten spec's storage-agnostic invariant says the CLI never branches on a backend's name, and adding `obsidian` to the deletion set is a name-aware code path; (2) `/aide:brain config` runs `cli/sync` after the integration's prose has scaffolded `brain.aide`, and `cli/sync` is the right surface to handle legacy-key migration because it has the bandwidth (it knows which integration is being wired and what the legacy keys mean for that integration). Moving the migration to sync keeps the cold-start CLI brain-blind. The user-visible cost is one extra MCP entry sitting in `.mcp.json` until `/aide:brain config` runs — acceptable, because that entry was already there before init ran and the CLI was never going to launch the obsidian server anyway.

- **The legacy `obsidian` key migration moves to `cli/sync` (not part of this plan).** This decision changes a behavior `cli/sync` did not own before — it is implementation drift relative to `cli/sync`'s current spec. This plan does NOT modify `cli/sync` because the delegation explicitly scopes this work to `cli/init`. The `cli/sync` migration is a follow-up plan, tracked here as a Decision rather than a `Prerequisites` entry because cold-start CLI removal can land independently — leaving the legacy `obsidian` key in `.mcp.json` is a no-op, not a broken state. The follow-up plan's job is to add `obsidian: "delete"` (or equivalent) to `cli/sync`'s entries map when the user runs sync after wiring an Obsidian brain. Until that lands, users with a pre-rework `obsidian` entry carry it forward harmlessly until they invoke `/aide:brain config`, which re-runs sync with the new entry shape and (after the follow-up) deletes the legacy key.

- **`--ide` flag-parsing remains an open todo.aide item, not a step in this plan.** The current code does not implement `parseIde`, the `--ide <choice>` flag has no effect, and the IDE deferral string is always present in `deferredCategories`. The rewritten spec lists `--ide` as a recognized flag and the good example shows `[created] .zed/settings.json` when `--ide zed` is supplied. Implementing `--ide` is not scope-creep into a different module — it is in-module work that this plan deliberately defers because: (1) the delegation explicitly scopes this plan to brain-removal; (2) `--ide` wiring requires its own design pass (Zed vs. VS Code vs. Cursor file targets, the IDE-application step's failure mode, the warning's IDE-omission behavior), all of which are out-of-scope; (3) `todo.aide` already tracks the gap (item 2), and that is the right surface to carry it. The IDE deferral string stays exactly as-is in this plan — it is the same string the current code emits, byte-for-byte. After this plan, `todo.aide`'s `--ide` item can be addressed in a focused follow-up plan that adds `parseIde`, the IDE-application step, and the deferred-categories logic that omits the IDE entry when `--ide` was supplied.

- **The `[exists]`-results-in-warning gap remains an open todo.aide item.** Item 1 in `todo.aide` says `exists` results should appear in the warning's Skipped files section per the spec's good example (every pre-existing file is named so the user can investigate drift). The current code filters `exists` out of `warningSkipped`. Closing this gap is a one-line filter change, but it is unrelated to brain removal and would re-shape multiple existing tests in ways the delegation did not approve. Preserving it as `todo.aide` item 1 keeps the plan's scope tight; the follow-up plan that addresses it is small and isolated.

- **`renderWarning` is unchanged in shape; only its input strings change.** The renderer is data-driven by design — input strings from `deferredCategories()` flow through `runInit` into `renderWarning` verbatim. That separation is what makes this rewrite a string-table swap rather than a renderer change. The JSDoc update in Step 8b is documentation-only; the renderer's logic is unchanged.

- **Tests stay split between mocked unit (`index.test.ts`) and real-FS integration (`index.integration.test.ts`).** Both files exist already and the split matches the testing playbook's two-tier model. The mocked layer covers the orchestrator's branching logic (every helper invocation, the per-file log composition, the `renderWarning` input shape); the real-FS layer covers the actual end-to-end disk state (no `.aide/config/` directory, `.mcp.json` mcpServers shape, idempotency at the file level). Adding a third tier would be over-engineering. Both files are full rewrites in this plan because the contract changed enough that mechanical fixture replacement would leave dead code and stale describe blocks.

- **The brain-wiring deferred string names `/aide` as the user-typed entry point, then `/aide:brain config` as the wiring surface.** This is the exact shape the spec's good example uses. The retired anti-pattern is naming `/aide` as the *wiring* surface itself (e.g., "the orchestrator will detect that your brain is not wired and walk you through configuration") — that violates the "directs the user to a removed slash command" undesired outcome because `/aide` does not own brain-wiring inline recovery. The new string says "open Claude Code and run /aide; on the first run, /aide:brain config will scaffold ..." — `/aide` is named as the literal command the user types in Claude Code, then `/aide:brain config` is named as the surface that does the wiring. Both surfaces ship; the user's fingers type one of them; the agent invokes the other. The regression guard in Step 6d-ix asserts the new prose is present and the retired prose is not.

- **`writeMcpEntry`'s preserved-count logic stays.** The current helper re-reads `.mcp.json` after writing to count non-managed servers and includes the count in the result message ("merged with 2 existing servers"). This plan keeps that logic but updates the `managedKeys` set to drop `brain` and `obsidian` — neither key is managed by the cold-start CLI anymore. The user-visible message stays informative ("aide MCP server entry (merged with 2 existing servers)") and the implementation stays simple.

- **No new helpers in this scope.** The plan reuses every existing import that survives the brain-removal pruning. No new orchestrator-level helper is needed — the change is helper deletion, not helper addition. Adding a helper purely to "branch the message" or "compose the deferred array" would violate the playbook's "extract helpers into subfolders only when they have independent reasons to exist" rule.
